    {{! Field-level security: x-klabis-* extensions on the spec property map 1:1 onto the
        annotations documented in the klabis-api-spec skill. They render as parameter annotations,
        same position as on the hand-written records (see backend-patterns: @OwnerId MemberId id).

        Bean Validation constraints (@Email, @Size, @Pattern, ...) come from the stock
        JavaSpring beanValidation partial so request-side schemas (e.g. GuardianDTO, shared between
        RegisterMemberRequest and MemberDetailsResponse) keep the same runtime validation the
        hand-written DTOs had.

        @NotBlank, @Past and @URL are the exceptions: OpenAPI has no keyword for any of them ("not
        blank", "strictly in the past", "a syntactically valid URL" — `format: uri` is documentation
        only and the generator emits no constraint for it), so they come from x-klabis-not-blank /
        x-klabis-past / x-klabis-url rather than the partial. A field that is both `required` and
        not-blank therefore carries a redundant
        @NotNull from the partial as well; that is harmless, and suppressing it would mean forking
        the stock beanValidation template too.

        A nullable property becomes JsonNullable<T>, giving a PATCH body the absent/null/value
        tri-state. This mirrors the stock pojo.mustache, which gates the same wrapper on
        openApiNullable + isNullable; the record form just has no setters to also guard. The spec
        must use the OpenAPI 3.1 spelling `type: [x, "null"]` — the 3.0 `nullable: true` keyword is
        not recognised in a 3.1 document and leaves isNullable false. The type is fully qualified,
        so model.mustache needs no extra import. }}
